course: dictionary
lesson: 13155
title: Giving up on purpose
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Forgo is a formal word. It means to choose to go without something you would like, or to give up a chance, on purpose. Look at the clues in the sentence: «Sam forwent his dessert so his sister could have it.» Sam wanted the dessert, but he chose to go without it, so forwent means chose to go without.
explain: Forgo often comes before words like a treat, a chance or payment. «She forgoes a chance to play so her team can rest.» The word chance tells you she is giving up an opportunity. Ask yourself: did someone give up something they wanted? If yes, forgo is probably the word.
explain: Forgo changes its form, just like other verbs. Today it is forgo or forgoes, in the past it is forwent, and with have or has it becomes forgone. «Mum has forgone her coffee to save money.» Notice how the spelling changes, but the meaning stays the same.
fact: Forgo means to choose to go without something you would like, or to give up a chance.
fact: Forgo is a formal word, so you will often meet it in stories, in speeches and in writing about choices.
fact: The forms are forgo, forgoes, forwent, forgone and forgoing: «He forwent his turn» and «He has forgone his turn» both mean he gave his turn up.
fact: Forgo is used in phrases like forgo a treat, forgo a chance and forgo payment: «The volunteer forwent payment for the work.»
fact: In church life, people may forgo treats during Lent, choosing to go without something they enjoy for a time.
exercise: «Sam forwent his dessert so his sister could have it.» What does forwent mean? | *Chose to go without | Asked for a bigger one | Lost it on the bus | Ate it twice
exercise: «Many people forgo sweets during Lent.» What does forgo mean here? | *Give up, going without | Buy lots of sweets | Share the sweets with everyone | Hide the sweets in a box
exercise: Find and copy the past-tense form of forgo: «She forwent the chance to win the race so her friend could run.» | race | friend | *forwent | chance
test: «The volunteer forwent payment for the work.» What does forwent payment mean? | Paid someone else for the work | Asked for double pay | Counted the money twice | *Gave up being paid
test: «Would you forgo a treat to save your pocket money for a bike?» What does forgo mean? | Lose your pocket money | Buy two treats | Hide the bike in the shed | *Go without a treat
exam: What does Tom do when he forgoes his turn on the swing so Lily can go? | He falls off the swing | *He gives up his turn | He swings higher than Lily | He asks for a new swing
